title: Protocol surfaces
---
%% Source of truth: every caller of felix.runtime:build_tenant_agent —
%% routes/chat.py, routes/openai_compat.py, a2a/server.py, mcp/server.py,
%% jobs/scheduler.py, eval/runner.py, durability/fibers.py
flowchart LR
    accTitle: Five protocols and two schedulers reach the same compiled agent
    accDescr: Felix serves the same manifest over REST and SSE, an OpenAI-compatible /v1, A2A JSON-RPC, and MCP, and the worker reaches it again for scheduled jobs, continuous eval, and durable fiber resume. None of them has its own agent construction: all seven call felix.runtime.build_tenant_agent, which is why a governance wrapper cannot be applied on one surface and skipped on another. The e2e suite boots the same zero-argument create_application production uses to keep that true.

    rest([REST + SSE<br/>/chat]) -->|"streams a turn as SSE"| rt
    oai([OpenAI-compatible<br/>/v1/chat/completions]) -->|"existing OpenAI SDKs, unchanged"| rt
    a2a([A2A JSON-RPC<br/>/a2a]) -->|"message/send, tasks/get"| rt
    mcp([MCP<br/>/mcp]) -->|"tools/list, tools/call"| rt

    cron["felix-scheduler<br/>scheduled jobs"] -->|"fires due cron rows"| rt
    ev["Continuous eval<br/>eval/runner.py"] -->|"scores a dataset against a version"| rt
    fib["Fiber resume<br/>durability/fibers.py"] -->|"resumes a durable run"| rt

    rt["felix.runtime<br/>build_tenant_agent"] -->|"compiles per request"| build[[Manifest compile pipeline]]
    build -->|"returns"| agent(["Governed Agent"])

    class rt focal
    class rest,oai,a2a,mcp external
    class cron,ev,fib,build,agent primary

    classDef focal    fill:none,stroke:#2F81F7,stroke-width:3px
    classDef primary  fill:none,stroke:#6e7781,stroke-width:1px
    classDef external fill:none,stroke:#7d8590,stroke-width:1px,stroke-dasharray:4 3
